{"entries":{"chapter-83-study-kidney-diseases|Chapter 83 Study: Kidney Diseases":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"ckd-definition-amp-diagnosis|CKD — Definition & Diagnosis":["u3"],"copy|CKD criteria: eGFR <60 mL/min/1.73 m² (normal young-adult GFR ~125; <15 = kidney failure), albuminuria (ACR >30 mg/g), hematuria (any degree), urinary casts, or imaging abnormality — any persisting >3 months (p1278).":["u4"],"copy|CKD is usually asymptomatic until GFR falls to roughly 15–20 mL/min/1.73 m² (p1278).":["u5"],"copy|US CKD prevalence ~11.5% overall (4.8% stages 1–2, 6.7% stages 3–5) but 47% in adults >70y (p1278).":["u6"],"ckd-management|CKD — Management":["u7"],"copy|BP target in CKD: SBP 120–130, DBP <90 (p1279); goal of <130/80 for diabetes + CKD (p1286).":["u8"],"copy|ACEI/ARB (RAS blockade) is the mainstay in diabetes when DN is suspected; do not combine ACEI+ARB (↑ complications) (p1279).":["u9"],"copy|SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 receptor agonists reduce CKD-progression risk in diabetics; benefit in nondiabetics is still emerging (p1279–1280).":["u10"],"copy|Statins (± ezetimibe) recommended once eGFR <60 (p1280).":["u11"],"copy|Low-protein diet (0.5–0.6 g/kg/d) or very-low (0.3–0.4 g/kg/d) — some evidence it may reduce progression to ESKD in CKD stage 4–5; in nondiabetic CKD stage ≤3 it does not appear to reduce progression (p1280).":["u12"],"copy|Discuss dialysis preferences once eGFR falls <~20 (p1280).":["u13"],"copy|Nephrology referral triggers (KCP list, p1278): AKI, red cell casts, refractory HTN, K⁺ abnormalities, recurrent/extensive stones, hereditary kidney disease, CKD stage 4–5, severely increased albuminuria. K/DIGO's own restated list (p1290) phrases the GFR trigger as GFR <30 instead.":["u14"],"aki|AKI":["u15"],"copy|Classified by urine output: nonoliguric >400 mL/day, oliguric 100–400, anuric <100 — nonoliguric has the better prognosis (p1280).":["u16"],"copy|Categorized prerenal / intrarenal / postrenal (Table 83-1, p1281–1282).":["u17"],"copy|NSAID use doubles AKI risk in adults >65 (p1282).":["u18"],"copy|CI-AKI (contrast-induced) risk factors: old age, CKD, diabetes, heart failure, volume depletion, other nephrotoxins (p1282).":["u19"],"glomerulonephritis-rpgn|Glomerulonephritis / RPGN":["u20"],"copy|IgA nephropathy is the most common primary GN worldwide; 30–40% progress to ESKD within 20 years; ESKD incidence is almost 2× higher in patients older than 50 (p1283).":["u21"],"copy|Postinfectious GN (PIGN): hematuria + proteinuria 10–14 days post-infection; hypocomplementemia in >70%; ~50% need acute dialysis; only ~20% achieve complete recovery, ~30% progress to ESKD (p1283).":["u22"],"copy|RPGN classification — Table 83-2 (p1284).":["u23"],"copy|Corticosteroids are the mainstay; cyclophosphamide for crescentic/proliferative disease or life-threatening complications (p1284–1285); rituximab is roughly equivalent potency to cyclophosphamide (p1285).":["u24"],"nephrotic-syndrome|Nephrotic Syndrome":["u25"],"copy|Definition: proteinuria >3 g/day + hypoalbuminemia + edema + dyslipidemia (p1285).":["u26"],"copy|Most common biopsy findings in older adults with NS: membranous nephropathy (MN) + minimal change disease (MCD) + amyloidosis = ~60% combined — MCD ~15%, MN ~30–40% of biopsies (p1285).":["u27"],"copy|Amyloidosis found on ~10% of biopsies done for presumed idiopathic NS (p1285).":["u28"],"copy|Treatment: RAS blockade for proteinuria; sodium restriction <2 g/day + loop diuretics for edema (p1286).":["u29"],"diabetic-nephropathy-dn|Diabetic Nephropathy (DN)":["u30"],"copy|~30% of diabetics develop DN in their lifetime; DN is the leading cause of NS and dialysis-ESKD in the US (p1286).":["u31"],"copy|Accelerated kidney-function loss once eGFR falls <45 mL/min/1.73 m² (p1286).":["u32"],"copy|ACEI/ARB, SGLT2i, and GLP-1 RA all delay CKD→ESKD progression; BP goal <130/80 (p1286).":["u33"],"acute-interstitial-nephritis-ain|Acute Interstitial Nephritis (AIN)":["u34"],"copy|~80% of older patients show partial/complete recovery within 6 months (p1287).":["u35"],"copy|Most cases in older adults are drug-induced (antibiotics, PPIs, NSAIDs, diuretics); stopping the offending drug is the key intervention (p1287).":["u36"],"copy|Corticosteroids improve outcomes if started within 14 days of first symptoms (p1287, main text).":["u37"],"copy|Rare: airway involvement can cause bronchospasm (p1287).":["u38"],"renovascular-disease|Renovascular Disease":["u39"],"copy|Prevalence 2–3% of general hypertensive population; up to 40% of refractory hypertension (p1287).":["u40"],"copy|Atherosclerosis accounts for almost 90% of renovascular cases in older adults (p1287), and a recognized nonatherosclerotic cause is fibromuscular dysplasia; Hazzard prints “fibrous dysplasia” in the paragraph and in Table 83-3 (p1288).":["u41"],"copy|Duplex ultrasound sensitivity/specificity: low-to-mid 90% range (p1288).":["u42"],"copy|Renal vein renin ratio ≥1.5 between kidneys predicts a beneficial BP response to revascularization; failure to lateralize does not predict a negative response; overall BP response cannot be predicted confidently with renal vein renin measurements (p1289).":["u43"],"copy|CORAL trial (947 patients): no benefit of revascularization vs. medical management (p1289).":["u44"],"copy|Up to 50% of stenoses worsen over time, at up to 5%/year (p1289).":["u45"],"eskd-epidemiology-amp-referral|ESKD — Epidemiology & Referral":["u46"],"copy|ESKD/kidney failure = GFR <15 mL/min/1.73 m² (p1289).":["u47"],"copy|~10–13% of adults in North America, Europe, and Asia are estimated to have some form of CKD (p1290).":["u48"],"copy|Historic untreated CKD decline rate ~7–10 mL/min/year (p1290).":["u49"],"copy|~60% of US ESKD patients have nausea/vomiting at the time dialysis is initiated (p1291).":["u50"],"renal-replacement-therapy-choices|Renal Replacement Therapy Choices":["u51"],"copy|Conventional hemodialysis: 3×/week, ~4 h/session; access options are AV fistula (best outcomes), AV graft, or temporary catheter (high bacteremia rates) (p1291–1292).":["u52"],"copy|AV fistula matures over ~3 months before it's usable (p1292).":["u53"],"copy|Peritoneal dialysis: continuous cyclic or nocturnal PD preferred in long-term care (p1293).":["u54"],"copy|Among patients >75 placed on a kidney-transplant waitlist, <40% receive a transplant within 5 years (p1293).":["u55"],"copy|A time-limited dialysis trial is typically predetermined at 4–6 weeks (p1293).":["u56"],"eskd-complications|ESKD Complications":["u57"],"copy|Anemia: typically develops around GFR ~35 mL/min; normochromic, normocytic (p1294). The chapter gives a conservative hemoglobin level near 11.5 g/dL and misprints the unit as mg/dL (p1294); KDIGO 2026, recommendation 3.3.1 advises an ESA treatment target below 11.5 g/dL in adults with CKD.":["u58"],"copy|Hazzard describes ferritin <500 ng/mL or transferrin saturation <30% under its historical “functional iron deficiency” wording (p1294). In anemic patients receiving hemodialysis, KDIGO 2026 recommendation 2.1 suggests starting iron when both ferritin is ≤500 ng/mL and transferrin saturation is ≤30%.":["u59"],"copy|Cardiovascular death risk on dialysis: 10–100× the general-population risk (p1294).":["u60"],"copy|Secondary hyperparathyroidism: PTH begins rising by GFR ~40 mL/min (p1295).":["u61"],"copy|Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) supplementation for malnutrition: 50 mg/day (p1295).":["u62"],"copy|Pruritus affects up to 40% of ESKD patients; low-dose gabapentin reduces uremic pruritus; avoid diphenhydramine/hydroxyzine (anticholinergic burden) (p1296).":["u63"],"prognosis-amp-end-of-life|Prognosis & End of Life":["u64"],"copy|1-year survival for octogenarians/nonagenarians after starting dialysis: 50% per the chapter's own Key Clinical Points (p1278) — see book contradiction below.":["u65"],"copy|87% of nursing-home residents died or had decreased functional status within 1 year of starting hemodialysis (p1296).":["u66"],"copy|Hospice use is 5-fold lower among hemodialysis patients than among older non-dialysis patients (p1297).":["u67"],"copy|The \"surprise question\" (would you be surprised if this patient died in 6 months?), combined with albumin, age, dementia, and peripheral vascular disease, is a validated mortality predictor (p1296).":["u68"],"drugs-name-dose-target-key-cautions|Drugs (name — dose/target — key cautions)":["u69"],"copy|Phosphate binders (calcium acetate, sevelamer, lanthanum carbonate) — for hyperphosphatemia not controlled by diet alone (p1280).":["u70"],"copy|Calcitriol (active vitamin D analog) — for PTH unresponsive to plain vitamin D supplementation; caution: hypercalcemia and hyperphosphatemia (p1280).":["u71"],"copy|Pyridoxine — 50 mg/day for malnutrition (p1295); gabapentin — low-dose, for uremic pruritus (p1296); avoid diphenhydramine/hydroxyzine in ESKD pruritus (anticholinergic burden) (p1296).":["u72"],"book-contradiction-exam-trap|Book contradiction (exam trap)":["u73"],"copy|1-year dialysis survival for octogenarians/nonagenarians is given as two different numbers in the same chapter: the opening Key Clinical Points box says 50% (p1278), but the \"Prognosis and Survival\" section later states 54% (p1296) for the identical statistic. Both are printed as-is; know which figure a question is quoting.":["u74"],"recall|Recall":["u75"],"copy|CHAPTER 83s / HAZZARD":["u76"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 83; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u77"],"copy|1.":["u78"],"copy|What eGFR, albuminuria, and duration criteria define CKD?":["u79"],"copy|eGFR <60 mL/min/1.73 m² (or albuminuria >30 mg/g, hematuria, urinary casts, or imaging abnormality), persisting more than 3 months (p1278).":["u80"],"copy|3.":["u81"],"copy|What blood pressure target is recommended for older adults with CKD?":["u82"],"copy|Systolic 120–130 mm Hg, diastolic under 90 mm Hg (p1279).":["u83"],"copy|4.":["u84"],"copy|At what eGFR does statin therapy become specifically recommended in CKD, and is combining ACEI with ARB advised?":["u85"],"copy|eGFR under 60 mL/min/1.73 m².":["u86"],"copy|Combining ACEI and ARB is generally not advised — it increases complications (p1279–1280).":["u87"],"copy|5.":["u88"],"copy|How is AKI classified by urine output, and which category has the best prognosis?":["u89"],"copy|Nonoliguric (>400 mL/day), oliguric (100–400 mL/day), anuric (<100 mL/day); nonoliguric AKI has the better prognosis (p1280).":["u90"],"copy|7.":["u91"],"copy|What proportion of IgA nephropathy patients progress to ESKD within 20 years, and how does age at onset affect ESKD risk?":["u92"],"copy|30–40% progress to ESKD within 20 years; ESKD incidence is almost twice as high in patients older than 50 (p1283).":["u93"],"copy|8.":["u94"],"copy|What three diagnoses account for about 60% of kidney-biopsy findings in older adults with nephrotic syndrome?":["u95"],"copy|Membranous nephropathy, minimal change disease, and amyloidosis (p1285).":["u96"],"copy|9.":["u97"],"copy|At what eGFR does diabetic nephropathy show accelerated loss of kidney function, and what is the BP goal in diabetes plus CKD?":["u98"],"copy|eGFR below 45 mL/min/1.73 m²; BP goal under 130/80 mm Hg (p1286).":["u99"],"copy|10.":["u100"],"copy|What proportion of older patients with acute interstitial nephritis recover partially or completely within 6 months, and what is the single most important management step?":["u101"],"copy|About 80%; the most important step is stopping the offending drug (p1287).":["u102"],"copy|11.":["u103"],"copy|What proportion of renovascular disease in older adults is due to atherosclerosis, and what did the CORAL trial show about revascularization?":["u104"],"copy|About 90%; CORAL (947 patients) showed no benefit of revascularization over medical management (p1287, p1289).":["u105"],"copy|13.":["u106"],"copy|Around what GFR does renal anemia typically develop, and what type of anemia is it?":["u107"],"copy|Around GFR 35 mL/min; a normochromic, normocytic anemia due to inadequate erythropoietin production (p1294).":["u108"],"copy|15.":["u109"],"copy|The chapter states two different 1-year survival rates for octogenarians/nonagenarians starting dialysis — what are they, and where does each appear?":["u110"],"copy|50% (Key Clinical Points, p1278) and 54% (Prognosis and Survival section, p1296) — the same statistic printed differently in two places in the same chapter.":["u111"],"copy|Answer":["u112","u113","u114","u115","u116","u117","u118","u119","u120","u121","u122"],"copy|Atherosclerosis accounts for almost 90% of renovascular disease in the geriatric population. Hazzard calls the other cause “fibrous dysplasia” in the prose (p1287) and Table 83-3 (p1288).Current practice (not the book): the accepted vascular diagnosis is fibromuscular dysplasia — International consensus, 2019.":["u41"],"copy|Anemia in CKD is typically normochromic and normocytic and develops around GFR 35 mL/min. Hazzard prints a conservative hemoglobin target “near 11.5 mg/dL” (p1294). [sic — hemoglobin is measured in g/dL here.]Current practice (not the book): in adults receiving an ESA, target hemoglobin below 11.5 g/dL — KDIGO 2026, recommendation 3.3.1.":["u270"],"copy|Hazzard describes ferritin <500 ng/mL or transferrin saturation <30% under its historical “functional iron deficiency” wording (p1294).Current practice (not the book): for anemic patients receiving hemodialysis, KDIGO 2026 recommendation 2.1 suggests starting iron when both ferritin is ≤500 ng/mL and transferrin saturation is ≤30%.":["u59"],"copy|CHAPTER 83 / HAZZARD":["u76"]},"maxId":270,"sequenceOffsets":[[271,-148],[418,0]]}
